Builds that include `coreav thumbnail` (the `thumbnail` feature, which the
`ffmpeg-fallback`, `dav1d`, `libvpx` and `native-h264` features turn on) encode JPEG
with the jpeg-encoder crate (https://github.com/vstroebel/jpeg-encoder),
licensed (MIT OR Apache-2.0) AND IJG:

This software is based in part on the work of the Independent JPEG Group.

Builds with the `dav1d` feature decode AV1 with dav1d
(https://code.videolan.org/videolan/dav1d), copyright VideoLAN and dav1d
authors, licensed under the BSD 2-Clause licence, with the Alliance for
Open Media Patent License 1.0. Builds with the `libvpx` feature decode
VP8 and VP9 with libvpx (https://chromium.googlesource.com/webm/libvpx),
copyright The WebM Project authors, licensed under the BSD 3-Clause licence, with
Google's additional IP rights grant for the WebM codecs. Builds with the
`libopus` feature encode Opus with libopus (https://opus-codec.org),
copyright Xiph.Org, Skype Limited, Octasic, Jean-Marc Valin, Timothy B.
Terriberry, CSIRO, Gregory Maxwell, Mark Borgerding, Erik de Castro Lopo,
Mozilla and Amazon, licensed under the BSD 3-Clause licence, with the
royalty-free patent licences of Xiph.Org, Microsoft and Broadcom that its
licence names. The release archives and the npm packages of the coreav
command link dav1d 1.5.3, libvpx 1.14.1 and libopus 1.6.1 into the
binary, and ship their licence files in licenses/: dav1d's COPYING and
PATENTS, libvpx's LICENSE, PATENTS and AUTHORS, libopus's COPYING, and
the ISC licence of the x86 assembly layer of dav1d and libvpx. Those
files are part of this notice. A build that links the system's libraries
ships none of them; a distribution that ships a library with the command
ships that library's files with it.

The full builds (the coreav-full release archives, and any build with the
`native-h264` or `aac` feature) decode H.264 and AAC with CoreAV's own
decoders. Decoding H.264 or AAC may require a patent licence, depending on
where you are and what you do with it. Nothing here is legal advice.

The Opus decoder (the coreav-opus crate, crates/coreav-opus) contains tables
that scripts/opus_tables.py generates from the code of RFC 6716 Appendix A,
with the changes of RFC 8251, whose decoder part is the normative
specification of Opus (RFC 6716 section 1); the decoder also matches that
code's SILK resampler. That code carries this notice and licence:

